# Klingenberg Food — environment variables (technical plan §10e)
#
# NAMES ONLY. This file is committed; it must never contain a real value.
# Copy it to `.env.local` (git-ignored) and fill it in locally.
#
# Real values live in:
#   - Vercel project settings              (application runtime)
#   - Supabase project settings            (auth SMTP)
#   - protected GitHub environments        (CI, and the phase-13 backup job)
#   - `.env.production.local` on the operator's machine, for the one-time
#     production commands — see "TWO ENVIRONMENT FILES" below
#
# Local Supabase values are printed by `npm run db:start` and are throwaway keys
# for a container on this machine — they are still not committed.
#
# --- TWO ENVIRONMENT FILES, NEVER ONE -----------------------------------------
# Both are matched by `.env.*` in .gitignore and neither is ever committed.
#
#   .env.local              The LOCAL Supabase stack, and nothing else. Read by
#                           `next dev`, `npm run db:users`, `npm run backup`,
#                           `npm run backup:restore`, and the drill and
#                           integration configurations.
#
#   .env.production.local   The PRODUCTION Supabase project, and nothing else.
#                           Read by `npm run launch:migrate`,
#                           `npm run launch:load-content`,
#                           `npm run launch:bootstrap-owner`,
#                           `npm run backup:production` and
#                           `npm run backup:restore:production`.
#
# No command loads both, on purpose. A production connection string in
# `.env.local` would put it into every development shell, every drill and every
# test run — which is exactly the mixed environment these two files prevent. The
# loading is done by Node itself (`--env-file-if-exists=…` in package.json); there
# is no dotenv dependency and nothing to export by hand in each terminal.
#
# A real environment variable always wins over either file, so a CI job that sets
# its own values (the backup and migration workflows do) is unaffected by both.
#
# Everything below is a name that may appear in EITHER file; the sections say which.

# --- Supabase -----------------------------------------------------------------
# Public by design. The anon key is read-only via RLS (§8).
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=

# Server only. Never prefixed NEXT_PUBLIC_. Read exclusively through
# lib/env/server.ts, which imports "server-only" (§8).
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=

# Never in a browser bundle and never on a command line (§8 — log hygiene): the
# tooling always receives it as an environment variable. Used for migrations, and
# by the phase-13 backup tooling (scripts/backup) as the source of a backup or the
# target of a restore. It has exactly one value per environment file, and the two
# are never the same connection:
#
#   .env.local             the DB_URL `npm run db:start` prints (loopback, 54322)
#   .env.production.local  the hosted project's SESSION pooler string, PORT 5432 —
#                          never the transaction pooler (6543), because pg_dump and
#                          the migration door both need a session
#
# In CI it comes from a protected GitHub environment instead (`backup`, `production`)
# and neither file is present.
SUPABASE_DB_URL=

# --- Site ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# The ONLY place a site domain may be configured. Everything absolute — canonicals,
# sitemap, robots, Open Graph, JSON-LD, Server Action allowed origins — resolves
# through lib/config/site.ts (§10d).
#
# Leave empty locally: it falls back to http://localhost:3000.
# Leave empty on Vercel until the domain exists: it falls back to the Vercel host.
SITE_URL=

# --- Rate limiting (phase 13B) ------------------------------------------------
# Server only. Keys the sign-in throttle's client subjects: the address a failed
# login came from, and the account it aimed at, are stored only as an HMAC under
# this secret (lib/rate-limit/subject.ts) — never in the clear. Any long random
# string of at least 32 characters (`openssl rand -hex 32`); rotate it and every
# open window simply starts over. Leave it empty locally (a fixed development key
# is used, also for a local production build). On Vercel it is REQUIRED: a
# deployment without it refuses every sign-in and reset request, naming this
# variable in the function log, rather than keying the throttle per instance.
RATE_LIMIT_SECRET=

# --- Monitoring (phase 13C) ---------------------------------------------------
# Server-side Sentry only. There is deliberately no browser SDK (§1, §12), so no
# NEXT_PUBLIC_ variable exists for it and never may. Server only, read through
# lib/env/server.ts. Optional everywhere: leave it empty locally and in the test
# runs (monitoring is then off; nothing fails). On Vercel, set it for Production
# to report unexpected server errors; leave Preview unset unless preview events
# are wanted (they carry environment=preview). See docs/runbooks/monitoring.md.
SENTRY_DSN=

# Optional, never secret: a release name of your own. On Vercel the release is the
# deployment's commit SHA (VERCEL_GIT_COMMIT_SHA) unless this is set; elsewhere,
# without it, events say "unversioned".
SENTRY_RELEASE=

# --- CI (from phase 3, once Playwright runs against protected previews) -------
# Vercel Pro enables Deployment Protection on previews; E2E needs this to reach them.
VERCEL_AUTOMATION_BYPASS_SECRET=

# --- Not set here -------------------------------------------------------------
# RESEND_API_KEY and AUTH_EMAIL_FROM are configured in Supabase project settings
# (custom SMTP), not in the application environment. Local development uses the
# Supabase CLI mail catcher instead (§10c).
#
# --- Backup (phase 13A, §10f) -------------------------------------------------
# The weekly off-platform backup runs in GitHub Actions from the protected
# `backup` environment, which holds SUPABASE_DB_URL, NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL,
# SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY and the destination below. None of these is needed
# locally; `npm run backup` against the local stack needs only the three Supabase
# values above. See docs/runbooks/backups.md.
#
# The destination: any S3-compatible private bucket (Cloudflare R2, Backblaze B2,
# …). REGION defaults to `auto`, PREFIX to `klingenberg-food`.
BACKUP_S3_ENDPOINT=
BACKUP_S3_BUCKET=
BACKUP_S3_REGION=
BACKUP_S3_PREFIX=
BACKUP_S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
BACKUP_S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=

# Restore safety (docs/runbooks/restore.md): a restore into anything but the local
# stack is refused unless this names the target's database host, exactly.
BACKUP_RESTORE_CONFIRM_HOST=

# Tooling knobs, never secret. BACKUP_PG_MODE is native|docker|auto (default auto:
# a PostgreSQL 17 client on the PATH, else the postgres:17 image). BACKUP_AWS_CLI
# names the AWS CLI executable when it is not simply `aws` on the PATH.
BACKUP_PG_MODE=
BACKUP_PG_IMAGE=
BACKUP_AWS_CLI=

# --- Launch tooling (phase 14A) — OPERATOR variables, never runtime ------------
# Three one-time production commands (scripts/launch/), each run from an
# operator's shell or, for the migrations, from the protected GitHub environment
# `production` (phase 14C). None of these is read by the application. They reuse
# the names above rather than adding new ones for the same values:
#
#   npm run launch:migrate         needs SUPABASE_DB_URL (session pooler, 5432)
#   npm run launch:load-content    needs SUPABASE_DB_URL
#   npm run launch:bootstrap-owner needs NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL and
#                                  SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY
#
# Each refuses the local stack, and each refuses a hosted project until its OWN
# confirmation names that project's host exactly (`<ref>.supabase.co`, as the
# Supabase dashboard shows it). One confirmation per operation, on purpose: a
# shell that confirmed a migration has not confirmed a content load or the Owner
# bootstrap. Set the one you need, for one command, and unset it afterwards.
#
# THESE THREE DO NOT BELONG IN `.env.production.local`. A confirmation that is
# stored is not a confirmation: a file that holds MIGRATE_CONFIRM_HOST has
# pre-confirmed every future migration run, which is the whole guard, defeated in
# one line. They are typed for one command and then gone:
#
#   PowerShell:  $env:MIGRATE_CONFIRM_HOST='<ref>.supabase.co'; npm run launch:migrate; Remove-Item Env:\MIGRATE_CONFIRM_HOST
#   bash/zsh:    MIGRATE_CONFIRM_HOST='<ref>.supabase.co' npm run launch:migrate
#
# The same holds for BACKUP_RESTORE_CONFIRM_HOST above.
MIGRATE_CONFIRM_HOST=
CONTENT_LOAD_CONFIRM_HOST=
BOOTSTRAP_CONFIRM_HOST=

# --- What belongs in `.env.production.local` (phase 14C) ----------------------
# PLACEHOLDERS ONLY, as everywhere in this file. Copy the names you need into the
# git-ignored `.env.production.local` and paste the real values there — never
# here, never into a chat message, never onto a command line (§8, log hygiene).
#
# For the FIRST production step (the migration run) exactly one variable is
# needed. Supabase dashboard → Project settings → Database → Connection string →
# **Session pooler**, which is PORT 5432. Not the transaction pooler on 6543:
# `pg_dump` and the migration door both need a session, and 6543 cannot give one.
#
#   SUPABASE_DB_URL="<PRIVATE SESSION POOLER URI>"
#
# The later production commands add, from Project settings → API:
#
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL="https://<ref>.supabase.co"
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY="<anon key>"
#   SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY="<service-role key>"
#
# And a production backup or restore adds the destination block above
# (BACKUP_S3_*) plus, on the machine that runs one, BACKUP_PG_MODE=docker when no
# PostgreSQL 17 client is on the PATH.
#
# `npm run backup:production` refuses to run at all unless SUPABASE_DB_URL and
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL resolve to the SAME project ref — a database from one
# project and Storage from another produces no recovery point rather than a
# misleading one (scripts/backup/lib/targets.mjs).
#
# Not in this file: the three confirmation variables above, and anything the
# application reads at runtime (those live in Vercel's project settings).
